Jada Pinkett Smith has revealed that she and husband Will Smith have been separated for the last seven years.

The Girls Trip star, 52, shared in a new interview with People published on 11 October that she and the King Richard actor, 55, are “still figuring it out” when it comes to their marriage of 26 years. She explained at the time of the 2022 Academy Awards, when Will slapped Chris Rock, they had been separated for six years.","entities":["Chris Rock","2022 Academy Awards","King Richard","separation","A timeline of Will Smith and Jada Pinkett Smith’s relationship amid separation","Jada Pinkett Smith","Will Smith","Girls Trip","The Independent","People"],"facts":["Jada Pinkett Smith and Will Smith have been separated since 2016 - when should couples just get divorced? -MENTIONS-> 2022 Academy Awards","Jada Pinkett Smith reveals why she and Will Smith never signed a prenup -MENTIONS-> Chris Rock","Jada Pinkett Smith and Will Smith have been separated since 2016 - when should couples just get divorced? -MENTIONS-> Chris Rock","Chris Rock -PARTICIPATED_IN-> 94th annual Oscars","Jada Pinkett Smith reveals why she and Will Smith never signed a prenup -MENTIONS-> 2022 Academy Awards"]}

{"title":"Is Google Search better than the rest? And is that fair? (The Age, 2023-10-22T18:00:00+00:00)","text":"Is Google Search better than the rest? And is that fair? (The Age, 2023-10-22T18:00:00+00:00)
The question remains, why do users prefer Google? And if the answer, as Walker says, is that Google is simply better, the question becomes whether that’s down to its scale, its longevity, its ubiquity and all the user data it sucks up, which no other company could possibly match.

For the US, which has to prove that Google somehow broke the law to achieve the status quo, this legal case could be an uphill battle. But for the rest of us, it offers a rare opportunity for some insight into what the tech giant does with its enormous market share. Is it still focused on being the best? Or, as has been alleged, does it use its ubiquity to squeeze us for money even at the expense of product quality? How ‘organic’ are Google’s results? This month, Wired posted an opinion article by lawyer and privacy advocate Megan Gray, which alleged that Google had accidentally revealed during the trial that it manipulates people’s search queries for maximum ad revenue.","entities":["Google Search","Wired opinion article","Megan Gray","Is Google Search better than the rest? And is that fair?","Walker","The Age","US","Google","trial","WIRED"],"facts":["Apple defends Google Search deal in court: ‘There wasn’t a valid alternative’ -MENTIONS-> Google Search","The people who ruined the internet -MENTIONS-> Google Search","Early impressions of Google’s Gemini aren’t great -MENTIONS-> Google Search","The restaurant nearest Google -MENTIONS-> Google Search","Deepfake election risks trigger EU call for more generative AI safeguards -MENTIONS-> Google Search"]}

{"title":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)","text":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)
In it, Bankman-Fried tried to justify the missing funds through the margin loan program. It was such a flimsy excuse that even George Stephanopoulos could see through it.

Did Bankman-Fried really think he could get away with it all? He must still think so, or he would not be dragging his closest companions’ names through the mud in a jury trial. Throughout the entire prosecution’s case, the defense has struggled to shake the credibility of witnesses such as Yedidia, Wang, and Ellison. The hits the defense made on Singh didn’t make me doubt the overall truth of his story — they just made me think that Singh was kind of self-serving and trying to secure himself a lenient sentence.","entities":["Bankman-Fried","In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way","Ellison","Singh","missing funds","FTX trial","margin loan program","George Stephanopoulos","the defense","FTX","Wang","Yedidia","the prosecution","The Verge"],"facts":["Sam Altman backs teens’ startup, Google unveils the Pixel 8 and TikTok tests an ad-free tier -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sex, Signal messages, and sabotaging FTX's code: SBF execs and Bahamas roommates tell all in court -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Today the FTX jury suffered through a code review -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Bankman-Fried -SAID-> Alameda Research"]}

{"title":"Sam Bankman-Fried didn’t ask where the $8 billion went (The Verge, 2023-10-31T19:52:07+00:00)","text":"Sam Bankman-Fried didn’t ask where the $8 billion went (The Verge, 2023-10-31T19:52:07+00:00)
He did. But — I did hear these words uttered aloud in a court of law this morning, I am not creative enough to make this kind of thing up — his employees told him “they were busy and I should stop asking questions because it was distracting.”

Yedidia — Bankman-Fried’s college friend, Bahamas roommate, and employee at FTX — had testified that he’d asked Bankman-Fried about the $8 billion hole on a padel tennis court in their luxury complex in June or July. Today, in testimony, Bankman-Fried seemed to be trying to deny that conversation had ever taken place. It was not until Judge Lewis Kaplan intervened to ask if Bankman-Fried had ever been told by Yedidia about that money, in words or in substance, that Bankman-Fried admitted he’d been told.","entities":["Lewis Kaplan","Sam Bankman-Fried didn’t ask where the $8 billion went","the $8 billion hole","Bahamas","FTX","Yedidia","Sam Bankman-Fried","The Verge"],"facts":["The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Lewis Kaplan","Sex, Signal messages, and sabotaging FTX's code: SBF execs and Bahamas roommates tell all in court -MENTIONS-> Lewis Kaplan","Lewis Kaplan -SAID-> market manipulation","Lewis Kaplan -PARTICIPATED_IN-> evidentiary hearing","Lewis Kaplan -PARTICIPATED_IN-> fraud trial of Sam Bankman-Fried"]}

{"title":"Some of our favorite devices are on sale for Black Friday. (The Verge, 2023-11-24T15:42:21+00:00)","text":"Some of our favorite devices are on sale for Black Friday. (The Verge, 2023-11-24T15:42:21+00:00)
Older buildings especially suffer from what I can only describe as loose socket syndrome, where those very convenient modern boxy socket extenders with five different USB and USB-C charging ports simply cannot stay in place and fall right out of the wall because they’re too heavy. After one (totally pleasant) vacation where I had to charge my phone, watch, AirPods, and laptop in a weird corner of my room with the plug-in charging hub propped up on a strategically balanced mountain of books and sham pillows, I bought this Anker combination extension cord / power strip. It’s not a full power strip — just a cube with a few sockets along with USB and USB-C charging ports at the end of a five-foot cable. I’ve brought it on a few trips since then. It takes up extra space in my suitcase but each time has left me feeling vindicated about the purchase.

The three prongs at the end of the cable are static, rather than folding flat for easy packing.","entities":["AirPods","Anker","Black Friday","Some of our favorite devices are on sale for Black Friday.","Anker combination extension cord / power strip","The Verge"],"facts":["The best Apple Cyber Monday deals of 2023: Save on AirPods, iPads, Apple Watches and more -MENTIONS-> AirPods","The best Apple Black Friday deals on iPads, AirPods, Apple Watches and more -MENTIONS-> AirPods","The 53 best Black Friday deals we could find at Amazon, Walmart, Target and more -MENTIONS-> AirPods","Here are over 40 last-minute gifts you can still nab before Christmas -MENTIONS-> AirPods","200+ of the best deals from Amazon's Cyber Monday sale -MENTIONS-> AirPods"]}

{"title":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)","text":"In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way (The Verge, 2023-10-26T12:00:00+00:00)
It is easy to lie with words and harder to lie with money. Bankman-Fried went around telling people that he wanted to make a lot of money to give it away, an idea of the Effective Altruism movement that Bankman-Fried affiliated himself with — but the amount of money he funneled to his Paper Bird vehicle alone was ten times the size of his philanthropic spending as outlined by Easton. And it was all vastly outweighed by his investments — the places where he could prove he was very smart by investing in the right companies early.

And in brief, explosive testimony, former FTX lawyer Can Sun demonstrated what Bankman-Fried’s tactic was. During the days in November when the exchange imploded, Bankman-Fried was trying to raise money from Apollo, the private equity group. Apollo wanted an explanation for what had happened, and so Bankman-Fried came to Sun to ask him to come up with justifications for the missing funds.","entities":["Easton","Bankman-Fried","In the end, the FTX trial was about the friends screwed along the way","Paper Bird","Effective Altruism movement","Can Sun","Apollo","missing funds","FTX trial","FTX","The Verge"],"facts":["SBF Trial: The latest updates from the FTX collapse’s courtroom drama -MENTIONS-> Easton","Easton -PARTICIPATED_IN-> SBF Trial","SBF’s trial starts soon, but how did he — and FTX — get here? -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sam Altman backs teens’ startup, Google unveils the Pixel 8 and TikTok tests an ad-free tier -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried","Sex, Signal messages, and sabotaging FTX's code: SBF execs and Bahamas roommates tell all in court -MENTIONS-> Bankman-Fried"]}

{"title":"The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-10-28T00:12:41+00:00)","text":"The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried (The Verge, 2023-10-28T00:12:41+00:00)
When he found it, he was “very surprised!”

Besides the two obvious lies Bankman-Fried told on the stand — about Alameda Research’s name and about “allow_negative” — I have been struck by how little he seems to know about his own companies. Apparently, Singh, Wang, and Ellison were out there just doing whatever their little hearts desired. Because Bankman-Fried was a CEO, but definitely not the kind that pays any attention to money at his crypto trading firm and futures exchange.","entities":["The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried","Alameda Research","allow_negative","Ellison","Singh","Wang","Sam Bankman-Fried","The Verge"],"facts":["The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Gary Wang","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Caroline Ellison","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Mark Cohen","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Adam Yedidia","The jury finally hears from Sam Bankman-Fried -MENTIONS-> Securities and Exchange Commission"]}

{"title":"Is Google Search better than the rest? And is that fair? (The Age, 2023-10-22T18:00:00+00:00)","text":"Is Google Search better than the rest? And is that fair? (The Age, 2023-10-22T18:00:00+00:00)
The example given was replacing a search for “children’s clothing” with “NIKOLAI-brand kidswear”. Loading Google rejected this in very strong terms, saying the piece was misleading and inaccurate while denying ever altering search terms. Wired removed the article for not meeting its standards, but the degree to which it was shared on social media and boosted in write-ups at other outlets shows just how willing people are to accept foul play on Google’s part. A suspicion of privacy invasion and inappropriate data handling follows the company at all times.

Grey herself is a former vice-president at DuckDuckGo, a privacy-focused company founded explicitly to counter giants such as Google. She admits that she may have misinterpreted the evidence, but maintains that Google manipulates Search to maximise ad revenue. So what does Google say? In a post on X, Google’s official search liaison, Danny Sullivan, said ad systems do not affect the organic results, i.e. the list of blue links in search results that are not sponsored.","entities":["Google Search","Is Google Search better than the rest? And is that fair?","Grey","DuckDuckGo","privacy invasion","inappropriate data handling","organic search results","ad systems affecting organic results","Danny Sullivan","X","The Age","Google","WIRED","ad revenue"],"facts":["Apple defends Google Search deal in court: ‘There wasn’t a valid alternative’ -MENTIONS-> Google Search","The people who ruined the internet -MENTIONS-> Google Search","Early impressions of Google’s Gemini aren’t great -MENTIONS-> Google Search","The restaurant nearest Google -MENTIONS-> Google Search","Deepfake election risks trigger EU call for more generative AI safeguards -MENTIONS-> Google Search"]}

